WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:10.198
Listen and practice speaking.

2
00:00:10.288 --> 00:00:12.419
Practice makes perfect.

3
00:00:13.808 --> 00:00:17.320
Hello and welcome to Business English Conversations.

4
00:00:17.339 --> 00:00:17.998
Hello everyone.

5
00:00:18.018 --> 00:00:22.277
Welcome back to the podcast where we practice real English for the workplace.

6
00:00:22.297 --> 00:00:24.312
My name is Emma, and my name is David.

7
00:00:24.332 --> 00:00:37.853
In this podcast, we help you learn how to communicate more confidently in professional situations, yes, especially situations that many people face at work, like meetings, presentations, interviews, and everyday conversations with colleagues.

8
00:00:37.874 --> 00:00:39.655
Today we have a very useful topic.

9
00:00:39.674 --> 00:00:43.720
Our topic today is how to explain a project you worked on in English.

10
00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:48.005
This is an important skill, because at work, people often ask about your projects.

11
00:00:48.024 --> 00:00:57.253
For example, during a meeting, a performance review, or even a job interview, someone might ask questions like, Can you tell us about a project you worked on?

12
00:00:57.274 --> 00:00:59.060
What was your role in the project?

13
00:00:59.079 --> 00:01:02.902
What challenges did you face, and what were the results of the project?

14
00:01:02.921 --> 00:01:08.310
In today's conversation, we will show you how to explain a project clearly and confidently.

15
00:01:08.329 --> 00:01:13.055
We will talk about the background, the goal, the process, the challenges, and the results.

16
00:01:13.075 --> 00:01:15.599
And we will use simple business English.

17
00:01:15.620 --> 00:01:16.302
So let's begin.

18
00:01:16.322 --> 00:01:19.584
Emma, have you worked on any interesting projects recently?

19
00:01:19.605 --> 00:01:20.757
Yes, actually, I have.

20
00:01:20.777 --> 00:01:26.376
A few months ago, I worked on a very interesting project at my company That sounds great.

21
00:01:26.396 --> 00:01:27.789
Can you tell me about it?

22
00:01:27.808 --> 00:01:28.609
Of course.

23
00:01:28.629 --> 00:01:32.715
The project was about improving our company's customer support system.

24
00:01:32.734 --> 00:01:35.055
That sounds like an important project.

25
00:01:35.075 --> 00:01:35.762
Yes, it was.

26
00:01:35.781 --> 00:01:42.555
Many customers had complained that our response time was too slow, so the company decided to make some changes.

27
00:01:42.575 --> 00:01:47.709
Our management team wanted to create a faster and more efficient support process.

28
00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.823
And what was your role in this project?

29
00:01:49.843 --> 00:01:51.704
I was the project coordinator.

30
00:01:51.724 --> 00:01:52.876
What did that involve?

31
00:01:52.897 --> 00:01:59.673
My main responsibility was to organize the project timeline and coordinate communication between different teams.

32
00:01:59.692 --> 00:02:01.784
Which teams were involved in the project?

33
00:02:01.804 --> 00:02:03.427
Several teams participated.

34
00:02:03.447 --> 00:02:07.879
We had the customer service team, the IT department, and the data analysis team.

35
00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.223
That sounds like a large collaboration.

36
00:02:10.242 --> 00:02:12.568
Yes, it was definitely a team effort.

37
00:02:12.587 --> 00:02:13.979
When did the project start?

38
00:02:13.998 --> 00:02:17.739
The project started in January and lasted about four months.

39
00:02:17.758 --> 00:02:25.781
That's a fairly long project, yes, because we wanted to carefully analyze the problem before implementing solutions.

40
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.010
That's very important in project management.

41
00:02:29.031 --> 00:02:29.549
Absolutely.

42
00:02:29.568 --> 00:02:33.310
If you rush the planning stage, you might create more problems later.

43
00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.073
So how did the project begin?

44
00:02:35.092 --> 00:02:38.836
First, we collected data about our customer service performance.

45
00:02:38.856 --> 00:02:40.848
What kind of data did you analyze?

46
00:02:40.868 --> 00:02:47.370
We looked at response times, customer satisfaction scores, and the number of support tickets per day.

47
00:02:47.389 --> 00:02:48.883
What did the data reveal?

48
00:02:48.903 --> 00:02:53.397
The data showed that our average response time was nearly 24 hours.

49
00:02:53.418 --> 00:02:55.659
That is quite slow for customer support.

50
00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:01.187
Yes, especially in today's fast-paced digital world, customers expect quick responses.

51
00:03:01.207 --> 00:03:04.700
Many customers expect replies within just a few hours.

52
00:03:04.721 --> 00:03:07.217
So what was the main goal of your project?

53
00:03:07.237 --> 00:03:11.473
Our main goal was to reduce the average response time to under six hours.

54
00:03:11.493 --> 00:03:13.242
That's a big improvement.

55
00:03:13.263 --> 00:03:13.998
Yes, it was.

56
00:03:14.019 --> 00:03:16.483
But we believed it was achievable.

57
00:03:16.502 --> 00:03:18.962
What steps did your team take to reach that goal?

58
00:03:18.983 --> 00:03:22.193
First, we identified the main reasons for the delays.

59
00:03:22.212 --> 00:03:23.937
What were the biggest problems?

60
00:03:23.957 --> 00:03:28.162
One major issue was that customer inquiries were not categorized properly.

61
00:03:28.181 --> 00:03:29.650
What do you mean by that?

62
00:03:29.669 --> 00:03:31.884
All tickets were going into the same queue.

63
00:03:31.905 --> 00:03:35.689
So simple questions and complex problems were mixed together.

64
00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:36.112
Exactly.

65
00:03:36.133 --> 00:03:39.336
This created confusion and slowed down the process.

66
00:03:39.356 --> 00:03:40.086
That makes sense.

67
00:03:40.106 --> 00:03:45.301
Another problem was that the customer service team didn't have enough automation tools.

68
00:03:45.322 --> 00:03:49.776
So many tasks had to be done manually, which made the process inefficient.

69
00:03:49.795 --> 00:03:51.764
What solution did your team propose?

70
00:03:51.784 --> 00:03:55.742
We proposed implementing a new ticket management system.

71
00:03:55.763 --> 00:03:57.233
What would that system do?

72
00:03:57.252 --> 00:04:01.705
It would automatically categorize customer inquiries based on keywords.

73
00:04:01.725 --> 00:04:03.444
That sounds very helpful.

74
00:04:03.465 --> 00:04:05.805
It allowed urgent issues to be prioritized.

75
00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.382
Who worked on building the new system?

76
00:04:09.401 --> 00:04:12.436
The IT team developed and configured the system.

77
00:04:12.455 --> 00:04:14.544
And what was your role during that stage?

78
00:04:14.563 --> 00:04:18.757
I coordinated meetings between the IT team and the customer service team.

79
00:04:18.778 --> 00:04:20.170
Why was that important?

80
00:04:20.189 --> 00:04:24.625
Because the IT team needed to understand the real needs of the support staff.

81
00:04:24.644 --> 00:04:27.641
Communication between teams is always critical.

82
00:04:27.661 --> 00:04:28.144
Absolutely.

83
00:04:28.163 --> 00:04:30.720
Were there any challenges during the project?

84
00:04:30.740 --> 00:04:31.427
Yes, several.

85
00:04:31.447 --> 00:04:33.302
What was the biggest challenge?

86
00:04:33.322 --> 00:04:35.875
One challenge was resistance to change.

87
00:04:35.896 --> 00:04:37.990
That happens in many organizations.

88
00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:41.274
Yes, some employees were comfortable with the old system.

89
00:04:41.293 --> 00:04:43.850
They were worried about learning a new system.

90
00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:46.428
Exactly how did you handle that challenge?

91
00:04:46.449 --> 00:04:49.708
We organized training sessions for the customer service team.

92
00:04:49.728 --> 00:04:51.821
What kind of training did you provide?

93
00:04:51.841 --> 00:04:56.036
We showed them how the new system worked and explained the benefits.

94
00:04:56.055 --> 00:04:57.850
Did that help reduce their concerns?

95
00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:58.153
Yes.

96
00:04:58.173 --> 00:05:02.958
Gradually, people usually feel more comfortable once they understand the benefits.

97
00:05:02.978 --> 00:05:03.636
That's right.

98
00:05:03.656 --> 00:05:05.331
Were there any technical challenges?

99
00:05:05.351 --> 00:05:05.670
Yes.

100
00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:10.149
Integrating the new system with our existing database was complicated.

101
00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:14.379
I can imagine the IT team had to spend extra time testing integration.

102
00:05:14.399 --> 00:05:17.365
Did the project timeline change because of that?

103
00:05:17.386 --> 00:05:20.103
Yes, we had a slight delay of about two weeks.

104
00:05:20.122 --> 00:05:23.088
That's not too bad for a technical project.

105
00:05:23.108 --> 00:05:25.826
Fortunately, management was very supportive.

106
00:05:25.846 --> 00:05:26.822
That always helps.

107
00:05:26.841 --> 00:05:30.305
They understood that quality was more important than speed.

108
00:05:30.324 --> 00:05:33.043
So after the system was ready, what happened next?

109
00:05:33.062 --> 00:05:34.783
We launched a pilot program.

110
00:05:34.803 --> 00:05:36.276
What is a pilot program?

111
00:05:36.297 --> 00:05:41.004
It means testing the new system with a small group before full implementation.

112
00:05:41.024 --> 00:05:42.747
That's a smart approach, yes.

113
00:05:42.766 --> 00:05:45.235
It allows you to identify problems early.

114
00:05:45.254 --> 00:05:47.475
Which team participated in the pilot?

115
00:05:47.495 --> 00:05:50.461
A small group from the customer service department.

116
00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.223
How long did the pilot phase last?

117
00:05:52.312 --> 00:05:53.882
About three weeks.

118
00:05:53.903 --> 00:05:54.843
What did you learn?

119
00:05:54.862 --> 00:05:59.403
During that time, we discovered some small issues with ticket categorization.

120
00:05:59.423 --> 00:06:01.050
Were those issues easy to fix?

121
00:06:01.071 --> 00:06:01.324
Yes.

122
00:06:01.345 --> 00:06:04.922
The IT team adjusted the system settings after the pilot program.

123
00:06:04.942 --> 00:06:07.565
Did you implement the system company-wide?

124
00:06:07.586 --> 00:06:11.648
Yes, we rolled it out to the entire customer support department.

125
00:06:11.668 --> 00:06:13.088
How did employees react?

126
00:06:13.108 --> 00:06:17.889
At first they were cautious, but soon they started appreciating the improvements.

127
00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.529
What kind of improvements did they notice?

128
00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:24.372
The system reduced their workload and helped them respond faster.

129
00:06:24.391 --> 00:06:26.773
That must have been satisfying for the team.

130
00:06:26.793 --> 00:06:28.934
Yes, everyone felt proud of the results.

131
00:06:28.954 --> 00:06:32.535
Speaking of results, what were the final outcomes of the project?

132
00:06:32.555 --> 00:06:34.216
The results were very positive.

133
00:06:34.235 --> 00:06:35.656
Can you share some numbers?

134
00:06:35.675 --> 00:06:39.495
Before the project, our average response time was 24 hours.

135
00:06:39.516 --> 00:06:42.619
And after the project, it decreased to about five hours.

136
00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:44.298
That's a huge improvement.

137
00:06:44.319 --> 00:06:46.220
Yes, we exceeded our original goal.

138
00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.139
What about customer satisfaction?

139
00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:52.463
Customer satisfaction scores increased by nearly 30%.

140
00:06:52.483 --> 00:06:53.434
That's excellent.

141
00:06:53.454 --> 00:06:53.920
Yes.

142
00:06:53.939 --> 00:06:56.103
The management team was very pleased.

143
00:06:56.124 --> 00:06:59.083
Did the company plan any follow-up improvements?

144
00:06:59.103 --> 00:06:59.528
Yes.

145
00:06:59.711 --> 00:07:03.492
We're now exploring the use of AI chatbots for basic questions.

146
00:07:03.512 --> 00:07:05.961
That could improve response times even further.

147
00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:06.312
Exactly.

148
00:07:06.333 --> 00:07:09.958
Looking back, what was the most valuable lesson from the project?

149
00:07:09.978 --> 00:07:14.088
I learned that communication is the key to successful teamwork.

150
00:07:14.108 --> 00:07:15.545
That's a great lesson.

151
00:07:15.564 --> 00:07:18.947
Without clear communication, the project could have failed.

152
00:07:18.968 --> 00:07:20.163
I completely agree.

153
00:07:20.184 --> 00:07:24.774
Also, involving employees early in the process helped reduce resistance.

154
00:07:24.793 --> 00:07:26.721
That's a very important point.

155
00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:29.637
People are more supportive when they feel included.

156
00:07:29.656 --> 00:07:33.283
If you had to do the project again, would you change anything?

157
00:07:33.303 --> 00:07:36.196
I would probably spend more time in the planning stage.

158
00:07:36.216 --> 00:07:36.930
Why is that?

159
00:07:36.951 --> 00:07:41.062
Because better planning could have prevented some technical issues.

160
00:07:41.081 --> 00:07:41.790
That makes sense.

161
00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:44.949
But overall, I think the project was very successful.

162
00:07:44.968 --> 00:07:46.889
It certainly sounds like it.

163
00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:50.624
Emma, have you ever had to explain a project during a job interview?

164
00:07:50.644 --> 00:07:51.264
What kind of questions do they ask?

165
00:07:51.285 --> 00:07:52.016
Yes, many times.

166
00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:56.911
Interviewers often asked me to describe a project and explain my role.

167
00:07:56.930 --> 00:07:58.024
That's very common.

168
00:07:58.045 --> 00:08:00.906
They also wanted to know about challenges and results.

169
00:08:00.925 --> 00:08:05.122
That's why it's useful to organize your explanation in a clear structure.

170
00:08:05.142 --> 00:08:07.312
First, explain the background of the project.

171
00:08:07.333 --> 00:08:08.687
Second, describe the goal.

172
00:08:08.706 --> 00:08:11.206
Third, explain your role and responsibilities.

173
00:08:11.226 --> 00:08:14.185
Fourth, talk about the challenges and how you solved them.

174
00:08:14.204 --> 00:08:16.934
And finally, share the results and what you learned.

175
00:08:16.954 --> 00:08:20.598
This structure helps your explanation sound clear and professional.

176
00:08:20.619 --> 00:08:23.348
It also helps the listener understand the full story.

177
00:08:23.367 --> 00:08:26.096
And that is exactly what employers want to hear.

178
00:08:26.117 --> 00:08:29.533
Before we finish today, we would like to ask you a question.

179
00:08:29.553 --> 00:08:31.595
Yes, here's today's question for you.

180
00:08:31.615 --> 00:08:34.115
Can you describe a project you worked on?

181
00:08:34.134 --> 00:08:38.467
It can be a work project, a school project, or even a personal project.

182
00:08:38.488 --> 00:08:42.361
Try to answer using this structure: What was the goal of the project?

183
00:08:42.381 --> 00:08:43.475
What was your role?

184
00:08:43.495 --> 00:08:44.865
What challenges did you face?

185
00:08:44.885 --> 00:08:46.256
And what were the results?

186
00:08:46.277 --> 00:08:49.464
Practicing this will help you improve your business English.

187
00:08:49.484 --> 00:08:51.525
Thank you so much for listening today.

188
00:08:51.546 --> 00:08:56.794
We hope this conversation helps you feel more confident when talking about your work experience.

189
00:08:56.815 --> 00:08:58.855
Keep practicing your English every day.

190
00:08:58.875 --> 00:09:04.355
And remember, communication is one of the most important skills in business — from both of us.

191
00:09:04.375 --> 00:09:05.745
Goodbye, everyone.

192
00:09:05.764 --> 00:09:06.761
See you next time.

193
00:09:06.782 --> 00:09:10.573
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to our English learning podcast.

194
00:09:10.594 --> 00:09:14.390
If you're joining us for the first time, thank you for being here.

195
00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:22.528
This podcast is designed to help you improve your English through natural conversations about real-life professional situations.

196
00:09:22.548 --> 00:09:23.293
That's right.

197
00:09:23.312 --> 00:09:32.703
Our goal is to help you become more confident when speaking English at work, especially in meetings, presentations, and professional discussions.

198
00:09:32.724 --> 00:09:35.755
I'm Speaker Two, and I'm here with my colleague.

199
00:09:35.775 --> 00:09:37.028
Speaker One, thanks.

200
00:09:37.048 --> 00:09:45.419
Today we're gonna talk about something extremely useful in the workplace, how to explain a project you worked on in English.

201
00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:47.708
Yes, this is a very important skill.

202
00:09:47.729 --> 00:09:52.793
Whether you are in a job interview, a team meeting, or a performance review.

203
00:09:52.813 --> 00:09:56.100
Or even a casual conversation with colleagues.

204
00:09:56.120 --> 00:10:00.169
You will often need to describe a project you worked on — exactly.

205
00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:06.715
Many professionals know their work very well, but sometimes they struggle to explain it clearly in English.

206
00:10:06.735 --> 00:10:12.393
They may have the technical knowledge, but organizing their explanation can be difficult.

207
00:10:12.413 --> 00:10:20.768
So today we're going to simulate a realistic conversation where one professional explains a project they worked on.

208
00:10:20.788 --> 00:10:29.187
During the conversation we will naturally demonstrate useful vocabulary, phrases and structures you can use in your own workplace conversations.

209
00:10:29.206 --> 00:10:30.676
Imagine this situation.

210
00:10:30.696 --> 00:10:35.365
Two colleagues are discussing recent projects during a weekly meeting.

211
00:10:35.385 --> 00:10:39.558
Speaker two asks speaker one about a project they recently completed.

212
00:10:39.578 --> 00:10:41.543
All right, let's begin the conversation.

213
00:10:41.562 --> 00:10:49.684
So during the last team meeting, you briefly mentioned that you had just completed a major project with the operations department.

214
00:10:49.705 --> 00:10:51.691
It sounded really interesting.

215
00:10:51.861 --> 00:10:54.880
We didn't have enough time to go into details.

216
00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:56.610
I'd love to hear more about it.

217
00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:58.830
Could you walk me through the project?

218
00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.068
Sure, I'd be happy to.

219
00:11:00.089 --> 00:11:06.817
It was actually one of the most challenging projects I've worked on this year, but also one of the most rewarding.

220
00:11:06.837 --> 00:11:12.451
The project focused on improving the internal workflow of our order processing system.

221
00:11:12.471 --> 00:11:13.996
That sounds important.

222
00:11:14.017 --> 00:11:17.068
What was the main problem you were trying to solve?

223
00:11:17.089 --> 00:11:32.710
Well, the main issue was that our order processing system had become inefficient over time as the company grew and the number of customers increased, the system we were using couldn't handle the volume efficiently, orders were sometimes delayed,

224
00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:37.836
and the operations team had to spend a lot of time manually correcting errors.

225
00:11:37.856 --> 00:11:38.351
I see.

226
00:11:38.371 --> 00:11:42.313
So the project was about making the process more efficient.

227
00:11:42.333 --> 00:11:42.710
Exactly.

228
00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:48.857
The goal was to streamline the workflow, reduce manual work, and improve overall processing speed.

229
00:11:48.878 --> 00:11:53.133
Management wanted us to analyze the existing system and propose improvements.

230
00:11:53.153 --> 00:11:56.548
Improvements that could save time and reduce errors.

231
00:11:56.568 --> 00:11:58.599
That sounds like a complex task.

232
00:11:58.619 --> 00:12:00.139
How did the project start?

233
00:12:00.158 --> 00:12:02.703
The project started with a planning phase.

234
00:12:02.724 --> 00:12:08.232
First, our team met with several departments, including operations, IT, and customer service.

235
00:12:08.253 --> 00:12:14.306
We wanted to understand how each department interacted with the order processing system.

236
00:12:14.326 --> 00:12:15.107
That makes sense.

237
00:12:15.128 --> 00:12:19.861
You needed to see the entire process before making changes—exactly.

238
00:12:19.881 --> 00:12:24.096
During those meetings, we asked many questions about daily workflows.

239
00:12:24.115 --> 00:12:30.706
We also collected data on processing times, error rates, and customer complaints related to order delays.

240
00:12:30.726 --> 00:12:36.253
So you were basically gathering information and identifying the main pain points?

241
00:12:36.273 --> 00:12:37.320
Yes, that's right.

242
00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:44.976
Once we gathered enough information, we created a process map that showed every step of the order processing workflow.

243
00:12:44.996 --> 00:12:46.043
A process map?

244
00:12:46.062 --> 00:12:47.893
Could you explain what that is?

245
00:12:47.913 --> 00:12:53.330
Of course, a process map is a visual diagram that shows how a process works.

246
00:12:53.350 --> 00:12:58.730
From beginning to end, it includes each step, the people involved, and the systems used.

247
00:12:58.750 --> 00:13:02.572
That sounds very helpful for understanding the big picture.

248
00:13:02.591 --> 00:13:03.831
It definitely was.

249
00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:06.710
When we mapped everything out, we noticed several bottlenecks.

250
00:13:06.730 --> 00:13:13.687
For example, certain approvals required manual verification, which slowed down the process significantly.

251
00:13:13.707 --> 00:13:14.225
Interesting.

252
00:13:14.245 --> 00:13:17.980
So once you identified the bottlenecks, what was the next step?

253
00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:22.240
After identifying the issues, our team moved into the solution design phase.

254
00:13:22.259 --> 00:13:25.745
We brainstormed several ways to improve the workflow.

255
00:13:25.764 --> 00:13:31.009
Some ideas involved automation, while others focused on simplifying certain procedures.

256
00:13:31.028 --> 00:13:34.759
Did you work closely with the IT department during this phase?

257
00:13:34.779 --> 00:13:35.767
Yes, very closely.

258
00:13:35.787 --> 00:13:41.525
Since many of the proposed improvements involved software changes, it played a crucial role.

259
00:13:41.546 --> 00:13:47.541
We had weekly meetings to discuss feasibility, development timelines, and system integration.

260
00:13:47.561 --> 00:13:51.818
That kind of collaboration is essential for a project like this.

261
00:13:52.422 --> 00:13:58.082
Without strong communication between teams, the project would have been much more difficult.

262
00:13:58.173 --> 00:14:01.320
I'm curious — what was your specific role in the project?

263
00:14:01.340 --> 00:14:05.511
My main responsibility was project coordination and process analysis.

264
00:14:05.532 --> 00:14:14.817
I worked closely with stakeholders from different departments to ensure that their requirements were understood and incorporated into the final solution.

265
00:14:14.836 --> 00:14:19.835
So you were acting as a bridge between the technical team and the business teams?

266
00:14:19.855 --> 00:14:20.169
Exactly.

267
00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:28.539
I had to translate business needs into technical requirements and also explain technical limitations to non-technical stakeholders.

268
00:14:28.559 --> 00:14:30.577
That can be challenging sometimes.

269
00:14:30.597 --> 00:14:31.801
It definitely can.

270
00:14:31.821 --> 00:14:35.986
One of the biggest challenges was aligning everyone's expectations.

271
00:14:36.006 --> 00:14:42.038
Different departments had different priorities, and sometimes their requests conflicted with each other.

272
00:14:42.057 --> 00:14:44.131
How did you handle that situation?

273
00:14:44.152 --> 00:14:51.106
We organized several alignment meetings where representatives from each department could discuss their concerns openly.

274
00:14:51.126 --> 00:14:58.322
During those meetings, we focused on finding solutions that would benefit the overall process rather than just one department.

275
00:14:58.342 --> 00:15:00.620
Like a very practical approach, yes.

276
00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:02.917
And it helped build trust among the teams.

277
00:15:02.937 --> 00:15:07.514
People felt that their voices were being heard, which made collaboration easier.

278
00:15:07.533 --> 00:15:10.325
Once the solution was designed, what happened next?

279
00:15:10.345 --> 00:15:12.875
The next phase was development and testing.

280
00:15:12.894 --> 00:15:19.264
The IT team began developing the new automated features while our team prepared the testing scenarios.

281
00:15:19.284 --> 00:15:21.562
What kind of testing did you conduct?

282
00:15:21.581 --> 00:15:24.371
We conducted several rounds of testing.

283
00:15:24.392 --> 00:15:27.436
First, there was internal testing by the developers.

284
00:15:27.456 --> 00:15:30.274
After that, we conducted user acceptance testing.

285
00:15:30.293 --> 00:15:35.854
where employees from the operations department used the system and provided feedback.

286
00:15:35.874 --> 00:15:38.817
User feedback is very valuable at that stage.

287
00:15:38.836 --> 00:15:39.187
Absolutely.

288
00:15:39.206 --> 00:15:45.049
In fact, we discovered several minor issues during user testing that we hadn't anticipated.

289
00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:51.447
For example, some screens were not very intuitive, so we had to redesign them to improve usability.

290
00:15:51.466 --> 00:15:55.393
That's a great example of why testing is so important.

291
00:15:55.413 --> 00:15:55.788
Exactly.

292
00:15:55.807 --> 00:16:00.342
Testing helps ensure that the final product actually meets users' needs.

293
00:16:00.363 --> 00:16:02.960
How long did the entire project take from start to finish?

294
00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:05.647
The project took approximately four months.

295
00:16:05.667 --> 00:16:08.573
The planning and analysis phase lasted about one month.

296
00:16:08.594 --> 00:16:14.673
The development phase took two months, and the final month was dedicated to testing and implementation.

297
00:16:14.692 --> 00:16:20.043
That's quite a significant project, yes, but the results were definitely worth the effort.

298
00:16:20.063 --> 00:16:22.482
I was just about to ask about the results.

299
00:16:22.503 --> 00:16:24.923
What were the final results of the project?

300
00:16:24.943 --> 00:16:29.799
After implementing the new system, we saw several measurable improvements.

301
00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:37.123
Order processing time decreased by about 30%, and the number of manual corrections dropped significantly.

302
00:16:37.143 --> 00:16:38.099
That's impressive.

303
00:16:38.119 --> 00:16:38.586
Yes.

304
00:16:38.606 --> 00:16:44.683
And customer satisfaction also improved because orders were delivered more quickly and accurately.

305
00:16:44.703 --> 00:16:49.833
It sounds like the project had a strong impact on both internal efficiency and customer experience.

306
00:16:49.854 --> 00:16:51.518
Exactly.

307
00:16:51.538 --> 00:16:54.437
That was one of the main goals from the beginning.

308
00:16:54.457 --> 00:16:58.359
Looking back at the project, what would you say was the most valuable lesson you learned?

309
00:16:58.379 --> 00:17:02.745
I would say the most valuable lesson was the importance of communication.

310
00:17:02.889 --> 00:17:09.223
When multiple departments are involved in a project, clear and consistent communication becomes essential.

311
00:17:09.243 --> 00:17:10.290
That's very true.

312
00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:12.780
Miscommunication can easily slow down a project.

313
00:17:12.800 --> 00:17:13.136
Yes.

314
00:17:13.156 --> 00:17:16.817
And another lesson was the importance of involving end users.

315
00:17:16.836 --> 00:17:21.637
Early in the process, their feedback helped us design a system that truly met their needs.

316
00:17:21.657 --> 00:17:26.244
If you had the chance to do the project again, would you do anything differently?

317
00:17:26.265 --> 00:17:27.625
That's a good question.

318
00:17:27.644 --> 00:17:31.297
I think I would involve the training team earlier in the process.

319
00:17:31.317 --> 00:17:37.740
When the system was finally implemented, some employees needed additional training to adapt to the new workflow.

320
00:17:37.759 --> 00:17:39.351
That's an interesting point.

321
00:17:39.371 --> 00:17:43.483
Change management is often overlooked in technical projects.

322
00:17:43.503 --> 00:17:49.231
Even if a new system is technically better, people still need time and support to adjust to it.

323
00:17:49.251 --> 00:17:52.461
Overall, it sounds like the project was very successful.

324
00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:58.361
Yes, it was a great experience and a valuable opportunity to collaborate with different departments.

325
00:17:58.381 --> 00:17:59.589
Across the company.

326
00:17:59.609 --> 00:18:01.220
Thank you for sharing that.

327
00:18:01.240 --> 00:18:08.461
I think many professionals will find this example very helpful when they need to explain a project they worked on in English.

328
00:18:08.481 --> 00:18:09.163
I hope so.

329
00:18:09.182 --> 00:18:13.367
When explaining a project, it's helpful to follow a clear structure.

330
00:18:13.387 --> 00:18:22.942
You can start by describing the background or problem, then explain the goal of the project, your role, the steps you took, the challenges you faced, and finally, the results.

331
00:18:22.962 --> 00:18:27.839
That structure makes your explanation much easier for listeners to follow.

332
00:18:27.859 --> 00:18:32.509
It helps organize your thoughts and ensures that you cover all the important points.

333
00:18:32.529 --> 00:18:36.019
Well, that brings us to the end of today's conversation.

334
00:18:36.038 --> 00:18:37.890
Thank you for listening, everyone.

335
00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:44.193
We hope this discussion helps you feel more confident when explaining your professional projects in English.

336
00:18:44.213 --> 00:18:51.665
And remember, the more you practice speaking about your professional experiences, the more natural and fluent your English will become.

337
00:18:51.685 --> 00:18:56.330
We'll see you again in the next episode with another real-world English conversation.

338
00:18:56.351 --> 00:18:58.426
Until then, keep practicing and keep learning.

339
00:18:58.676 --> 00:19:03.876
Hello everyone, and welcome back to our English learning podcast.

340
00:19:03.896 --> 00:19:10.948
Today we are going to talk about something extremely useful for students, professionals and anyone learning English.

341
00:19:10.968 --> 00:19:15.097
The topic is how to explain a project you worked on in English.

342
00:19:15.117 --> 00:19:17.048
This situation happens very often.

343
00:19:17.067 --> 00:19:23.628
Maybe you are in a job interview and the interviewer asks, Can you tell me about a project you worked on?

344
00:19:23.648 --> 00:19:29.979
Or maybe you were in a meeting, a presentation, or even a casual conversation with colleagues.

345
00:19:29.999 --> 00:19:38.028
Someone might ask you to describe something you worked on, and suddenly it becomes difficult to organize your thoughts in English.

346
00:19:38.048 --> 00:19:39.249
Yes, that's very common.

347
00:19:39.269 --> 00:19:44.605
English learners know their projects very well because they spend a lot of time working on them.

348
00:19:44.625 --> 00:19:49.005
But when they try to explain them in English, they feel nervous or confused.

349
00:19:49.025 --> 00:19:54.368
They are not sure where to start, what details to include, or how to organize their explanation.

350
00:19:54.388 --> 00:19:59.009
The good news is that explaining a project does not need to be complicated.

351
00:19:59.029 --> 00:20:02.508
In fact, the key is simply to tell the story of the project clearly.

352
00:20:02.528 --> 00:20:05.479
I like that idea—telling the story of the project.

353
00:20:05.499 --> 00:20:07.700
A project really is like a story.

354
00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:10.667
It has a beginning, a middle, and an end.

355
00:20:10.688 --> 00:20:15.108
When you think of it this way, explaining a project becomes much easier.

356
00:20:15.128 --> 00:20:18.569
You simply guide the listener through what happened exactly.

357
00:20:18.589 --> 00:20:24.250
Many people try to explain everything about the project, including small technical details.

358
00:20:24.269 --> 00:20:26.969
But that often makes the explanation confusing.

359
00:20:26.989 --> 00:20:30.673
Instead, it is better to focus on the most important parts.

360
00:20:30.693 --> 00:20:36.098
Think about the project as something you were sharing with someone who knows nothing about it.

361
00:20:36.118 --> 00:20:40.295
Your job is to help them understand what you did and why it mattered.

362
00:20:40.315 --> 00:20:42.523
That's a great way to think about it.

363
00:20:42.544 --> 00:20:49.439
Also, it's important to remember that when people ask about a project, they usually want to learn a few key things.

364
00:20:49.459 --> 00:20:58.358
They want to know what the project was about, why it was important, what you personally did, what difficulties you faced and what the final outcome was.

365
00:20:58.445 --> 00:21:04.743
And when you explain those points clearly, the listener can easily understand the entire experience.

366
00:21:04.763 --> 00:21:10.912
Another important thing to remember is that you don't need very complicated English to explain a project.

367
00:21:10.932 --> 00:21:15.182
Well, simple, clear sentences are usually much better than long, complex ones.

368
00:21:15.201 --> 00:21:18.266
That's a really important message for learners.

369
00:21:18.286 --> 00:21:26.806
Sometimes people think they need advanced vocabulary to sound professional, but often the best explanations are the ones that are simple and clear.

370
00:21:26.826 --> 00:21:31.289
If someone understands you easily, you are communicating successfully.

371
00:21:31.309 --> 00:21:31.788
Exactly.

372
00:21:31.807 --> 00:21:33.449
Communication is the goal.

373
00:21:33.469 --> 00:21:38.193
When you describe a project, you are not trying to impress people with big words.

374
00:21:38.213 --> 00:21:42.938
You are trying to explain an experience in a way that is easy to follow.

375
00:21:42.958 --> 00:21:45.073
Let's imagine a simple situation.

376
00:21:45.093 --> 00:21:49.344
Suppose someone asks you, 'Tell me about a project you worked on recently.'

377
00:21:49.364 --> 00:21:52.387
Many people might pause and feel unsure about how to begin.

378
00:21:52.407 --> 00:21:52.664
Yes.

379
00:21:52.684 --> 00:21:58.621
And that pause usually happens because people are trying to organize everything in their heads at the same time.

380
00:21:58.712 --> 00:22:04.163
They're thinking about the details, the timeline, the results, and their role in the project.

381
00:22:04.182 --> 00:22:08.765
But a much easier approach is to start with a simple introduction, right?

382
00:22:08.785 --> 00:22:23.767
You can begin with a short sentence that introduces the project, something like, 'Last year I worked on a project to improve our company's website.', or maybe, 'During my university studies, I worked on a research project about renewable energy.'

383
00:22:23.787 --> 00:22:29.082
Those kinds of sentences immediately give the listener a clear idea of what the project was.

384
00:22:29.102 --> 00:22:35.386
Once the listener understands the general topic, they can follow the rest of the explanation much more easily.

385
00:22:35.406 --> 00:22:39.494
Another good thing about starting this way is that it gives you confidence.

386
00:22:39.515 --> 00:22:45.782
When you say the first sentence clearly, your mind begins to relax, and it becomes easier to continue speaking.

387
00:22:45.803 --> 00:22:49.178
That's why many speakers struggle only at the beginning.

388
00:22:49.198 --> 00:22:52.568
Once they start talking, their ideas begin to flow more naturally.

389
00:22:52.588 --> 00:22:55.228
It's similar to starting a conversation.

390
00:22:55.248 --> 00:22:57.429
The first sentence is often the hardest.

391
00:22:58.942 --> 00:23:00.114
of the project.

392
00:23:00.134 --> 00:23:05.326
When people hear about a project, they naturally want to understand why it existed.

393
00:23:05.346 --> 00:23:07.565
What problem were you trying to solve?

394
00:23:07.585 --> 00:23:09.955
What improvement were you trying to make?

395
00:23:09.976 --> 00:23:10.298
Yes.

396
00:23:10.317 --> 00:23:13.771
And explaining the purpose gives meaning to the project.

397
00:23:13.791 --> 00:23:18.982
For example, imagine someone says, 'I worked on a project to redesign a website.'

398
00:23:19.002 --> 00:23:21.470
That sentence tells us what the project was, but we still don't know why it was necessary.

399
00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:24.701
Exactly.

400
00:23:24.721 --> 00:23:32.894
But if they continue by saying, 'The goal was to make the website easier for customers to use.', suddenly the project makes much more sense.

401
00:23:32.913 --> 00:23:33.640
That's right.

402
00:23:33.660 --> 00:23:36.619
The purpose connects the project to a real need.

403
00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:43.566
Maybe the company wanted to attract more customers, or maybe users were having difficulty navigating the website.

404
00:23:43.586 --> 00:23:49.031
When you explain the purpose, the listener understands the motivation behind the project.

405
00:23:49.051 --> 00:23:56.239
Now let's talk about something that is very important in professional conversations: describing your personal role.

406
00:23:56.259 --> 00:23:58.708
Yes, this is especially important during job interviews.

407
00:23:58.728 --> 00:24:04.517
Interviewers often ask about projects because they want to understand how you contribute to a team.

408
00:24:04.537 --> 00:24:09.642
If you only say what the team did, they may not clearly see your individual contribution.

409
00:24:09.662 --> 00:24:16.617
So instead of saying 'we created a new system', it can be helpful to explain your specific responsibility exactly.

410
00:24:16.637 --> 00:24:28.486
For example, you might say, 'My role in the project was to analyze customer feedback and identify areas for improvement', or 'I was responsible for coordinating communication between different departments.'

411
00:24:28.506 --> 00:24:31.974
Those kinds of statements show ownership and responsibility.

412
00:24:31.994 --> 00:24:40.349
They help the listener understand what skills you used during the project, and it's completely normal to use the word 'I' when talking about your role.

413
00:24:40.369 --> 00:24:46.162
Some people feel shy about doing that, but— It's important because you were describing your own experience.

414
00:24:46.182 --> 00:24:53.367
Another interesting part of explaining a project is talking about the challenges or difficulties you faced along the way.

415
00:24:53.387 --> 00:24:57.137
Yes, and challenges actually make your story more interesting.

416
00:24:57.157 --> 00:24:59.403
Every project has some kind of difficulty.

417
00:24:59.630 --> 00:25:04.758
Maybe there was a tight deadline, limited resources, or unexpected technical problems.

418
00:25:04.778 --> 00:25:10.232
When you talk about challenges, you also have the opportunity to show how you solved problems effectively.

419
00:25:10.252 --> 00:25:18.336
For example, someone might say, 'One challenge we faced was that the deadline was very short.', So we had to prioritize the most important tasks.

420
00:25:18.356 --> 00:25:23.332
That sentence shows that the team had to think carefully and make decisions under pressure.

421
00:25:23.352 --> 00:25:27.863
Another example might be communication challenges in many organizations.

422
00:25:27.883 --> 00:25:30.480
Different departments need to work together.

423
00:25:30.500 --> 00:25:33.104
And sometimes misunderstandings happen.

424
00:25:33.124 --> 00:25:35.486
Yes, and in that case, someone might explain.

425
00:25:35.506 --> 00:25:37.627
At the beginning of the project.

426
00:25:37.647 --> 00:25:40.249
Communication between teams was difficult.

427
00:25:40.269 --> 00:25:44.058
So we started having weekly meetings to keep everyone updated.

428
00:25:44.078 --> 00:25:46.200
This shows initiative and leadership.

429
00:25:46.220 --> 00:25:50.488
I like how these examples focus on not only the problem, but also the solution.

430
00:25:50.508 --> 00:25:51.679
That's the key point.

431
00:25:51.699 --> 00:25:55.153
When you talk about challenges, always explain what action you took.

432
00:25:55.363 --> 00:25:59.392
The listener wants to see how you respond to.

433
00:26:00.614 --> 00:26:03.240
Finally, we come to the result of the project.

434
00:26:03.259 --> 00:26:06.989
This is the moment where you explain what happened at the end.

435
00:26:07.009 --> 00:26:09.741
Yes, the result shows the impact of the work.

436
00:26:09.761 --> 00:26:15.246
Maybe the project improved efficiency, increased sales, or helped customers in some way.

437
00:26:15.266 --> 00:26:19.494
When possible, it's helpful to include numbers or measurable outcomes.

438
00:26:19.515 --> 00:26:22.000
Numbers can make the result more powerful.

439
00:26:22.019 --> 00:26:28.502
For example, someone might say, 'After launching the new website, online sales increased by 15%.’

440
00:26:28.522 --> 00:26:32.003
That kind of information gives a clear picture of success.

441
00:26:32.023 --> 00:26:37.255
Even if you don't have numbers, you can still describe the outcome in a meaningful way.

442
00:26:37.275 --> 00:26:42.757
For example, you might say the project helped improve communication between departments.

443
00:26:42.777 --> 00:26:45.501
Or we received very positive feedback from users.

444
00:26:45.521 --> 00:26:46.259
At this point.

445
00:26:46.278 --> 00:26:50.002
The listener understands the entire journey of the project.

446
00:26:50.022 --> 00:26:51.009
How it started.

447
00:26:51.029 --> 00:26:51.760
What you did.

448
00:26:51.780 --> 00:26:53.260
What challenges appeared.

449
00:26:53.280 --> 00:26:54.184
And what happened in the end?

450
00:26:54.204 --> 00:26:55.191
And when you practice explaining projects this way, you become more confident.

451
00:26:55.211 --> 00:26:55.532
Exactly.

452
00:26:58.686 --> 00:27:04.192
Each time you do it, over time, it becomes a natural part of your communication.

453
00:27:04.212 --> 00:27:08.405
Another useful tip is to practice explaining your projects out loud.

454
00:27:08.425 --> 00:27:15.098
Many learners only think about the explanation in their minds, but speaking out loud helps you organize your ideas more clearly.

455
00:27:15.118 --> 00:27:16.842
That's a great suggestion.

456
00:27:16.862 --> 00:27:20.808
You can practice alone, record yourself, or practice with a friend.

457
00:27:20.828 --> 00:27:26.018
The goal is simply to become comfortable describing your experiences in English.

458
00:27:26.038 --> 00:27:33.453
You might even practice explaining different kinds of projects, a work project, a school project, or a personal project.

459
00:27:33.473 --> 00:27:35.939
Yes, personal projects are great examples too.

460
00:27:35.959 --> 00:27:41.887
Maybe you organized an event, created a website, started a small business, or learned a new skill.

461
00:27:41.907 --> 00:27:46.847
All of these experiences can be explained using the same storytelling approach.

462
00:27:46.867 --> 00:27:54.059
And the more you practice telling these stories, the easier it becomes to speak naturally during real conversations.

463
00:27:55.237 --> 00:27:58.700
That's because practice builds confidence, and confidence makes communication smoother.

464
00:27:58.932 --> 00:28:04.184
Today's episode, I want to remind our listeners of one very important idea.

465
00:28:04.204 --> 00:28:08.407
When you explain a project in English, your goal is not perfection.

466
00:28:08.428 --> 00:28:09.798
Your goal is clarity.

467
00:28:09.817 --> 00:28:10.145
Exactly.

468
00:28:10.165 --> 00:28:13.369
You don't need perfect grammar or advanced vocabulary.

469
00:28:13.390 --> 00:28:18.824
What matters most is that the listener understands your story and the value of the work you did.

470
00:28:18.844 --> 00:28:26.267
So remember to stay calm, Organize your thoughts and explain your experience step by step, and most importantly, keep practicing.

471
00:28:26.287 --> 00:28:30.730
Every time you talk about a project, you become a stronger communicator.

472
00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:32.962
Thank you so much for joining us today.

473
00:28:32.982 --> 00:28:40.144
We hope this conversation helps you feel more confident the next time someone asks you about a project you worked on.

474
00:28:40.164 --> 00:28:47.092
Yes, thank you for listening, and we look forward to sharing another English learning conversation with you soon.

475
00:28:47.112 --> 00:28:51.067
Until next time, keep practicing, keep learning, and keep speaking English.

476
00:28:51.087 --> 00:28:51.811
Hi everyone.

477
00:28:51.832 --> 00:28:54.291
We are very happy you are here with us today.

478
00:28:54.311 --> 00:28:55.037
Hello, everyone.

479
00:28:55.057 --> 00:28:57.943
My name is Daniel, and my name is Erica.

480
00:28:57.963 --> 00:29:02.317
Welcome back to our business English conversation channel.

481
00:29:02.337 --> 00:29:08.435
On this channel, we help you practice English for work and professional situations.

482
00:29:08.455 --> 00:29:12.407
Yes, simple and useful English that you can use in meetings, presentations and daily business communication.

483
00:29:12.428 --> 00:29:17.965
If you enjoy learning with us, please like this video and subscribe to our channel.

484
00:29:17.986 --> 00:29:21.298
It really helps us create more lessons for you.

485
00:29:21.318 --> 00:29:21.519
Thank you so much.

486
00:29:21.538 --> 00:29:24.454
Today, we are going to talk about an important business topic.

487
00:29:24.474 --> 00:29:25.337
That's right.

488
00:29:25.357 --> 00:29:30.867
Today we will discuss how to explain a project you worked on in English.

489
00:29:30.887 --> 00:29:33.786
This is a very useful skill in business.

490
00:29:33.806 --> 00:29:37.865
Many jobs require you to talk about projects, tasks and results.

491
00:29:37.884 --> 00:29:43.686
For example, in interviews, meetings or reports, you may need to describe your work.

492
00:29:43.707 --> 00:29:49.517
So today we will practice simple and professional ways to explain a project.

493
00:29:49.537 --> 00:29:53.887
We will use easy English so you can understand and practice.

494
00:29:53.907 --> 00:29:55.057
Let's get started.

495
00:29:55.076 --> 00:29:56.828
Daniel, how are you today?

496
00:29:56.848 --> 00:29:57.588
I'm doing well.

497
00:29:57.608 --> 00:29:58.115
Thank you.

498
00:29:59.231 --> 00:30:02.166
Busy, but it's a good kind of busy — the kind that sounds productive.

499
00:30:02.270 --> 00:30:05.945
Being busy usually means we are working on important tasks.

500
00:30:05.965 --> 00:30:09.238
Yes, I was working on a small project this morning.

501
00:30:09.258 --> 00:30:10.758
Oh, that's interesting.

502
00:30:10.778 --> 00:30:12.530
What kind of project was it?

503
00:30:12.550 --> 00:30:14.557
It was a marketing project.

504
00:30:14.576 --> 00:30:20.127
We were creating ideas for a new advertising campaign — that sounds like important work.

505
00:30:20.147 --> 00:30:24.940
It is — marketing helps businesses reach customers and communicate their value.

506
00:30:24.960 --> 00:30:27.932
So today, maybe you can explain that project to us.

507
00:30:27.952 --> 00:30:28.231
Sure.

508
00:30:28.251 --> 00:30:30.765
I can explain it in simple English.

509
00:30:30.785 --> 00:30:33.297
That would be great practice for our viewers.

510
00:30:33.317 --> 00:30:34.057
Okay, let's begin.

511
00:30:34.076 --> 00:30:36.083
First, tell us the basic information.

512
00:30:36.103 --> 00:30:37.602
What was the project about?

513
00:30:37.622 --> 00:30:42.161
The project was about creating an advertising campaign for a new product.

514
00:30:42.181 --> 00:30:43.426
What was the product?

515
00:30:43.446 --> 00:30:47.731
It was a mobile application that helps people organize their daily tasks.

516
00:30:47.751 --> 00:30:48.872
That sounds useful.

517
00:30:48.892 --> 00:30:49.251
Yes.

518
00:30:49.271 --> 00:30:54.150
The idea was to show customers how the application can save time and improve productivity.

519
00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:58.739
So your goal was to promote the product and explain its benefits.

520
00:30:58.759 --> 00:31:00.859
Marketing is about communication and value.

521
00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:01.615
That makes sense.

522
00:31:01.634 --> 00:31:05.890
Businesses need to explain why customers should use their products.

523
00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:08.153
Let's talk about your role in the project.

524
00:31:08.173 --> 00:31:09.159
What did you do?

525
00:31:09.179 --> 00:31:12.428
My role was to create ideas for the advertisement.

526
00:31:12.448 --> 00:31:14.628
Can you explain that in more detail?

527
00:31:14.648 --> 00:31:14.942
Sure.

528
00:31:14.962 --> 00:31:19.470
I researched customer preferences and suggested marketing messages.

529
00:31:19.490 --> 00:31:21.481
What does ‘marketing message’ mean?

530
00:31:21.501 --> 00:31:23.996
A marketing message is the main idea.

531
00:31:24.016 --> 00:31:29.277
You want customers to understand, for example, why the product is useful.

532
00:31:29.297 --> 00:31:34.055
In our project, the message was that the application helps people save time.

533
00:31:34.075 --> 00:31:35.816
That sounds clear and simple.

534
00:31:35.836 --> 00:31:38.833
Yes, clear messages are important in marketing.

535
00:31:38.853 --> 00:31:40.845
Did you work alone or with a team?

536
00:31:40.865 --> 00:31:42.354
I worked with a team.

537
00:31:42.374 --> 00:31:45.372
Teamwork is very important in business projects.

538
00:31:45.392 --> 00:31:48.501
I agree — different people bring different ideas.

539
00:31:48.521 --> 00:31:48.893
Exactly.

540
00:31:48.913 --> 00:31:52.182
Our team included designers, writers, and marketing specialists.

541
00:31:54.103 --> 00:31:56.030
What did the designers do?

542
00:31:56.050 --> 00:31:58.921
The designers created visuals for the advertisement.

543
00:31:59.655 --> 00:32:01.672
Images and design elements, right?

544
00:32:01.692 --> 00:32:02.078
Yes.

545
00:32:02.098 --> 00:32:05.516
Visuals help attract attention and communicate ideas.

546
00:32:05.536 --> 00:32:07.101
What did the writers do?

547
00:32:07.121 --> 00:32:12.434
The writers created text for the advertisement, like slogans and descriptions.

548
00:32:12.454 --> 00:32:12.920
Exactly.

549
00:32:12.940 --> 00:32:17.150
A slogan is a short phrase that communicates a message.

550
00:32:17.170 --> 00:32:19.263
For example: 'Save time, live better.'

551
00:32:19.284 --> 00:32:20.849
That is a good example.

552
00:32:20.869 --> 00:32:24.284
Short and memorable slogans work well in marketing.

553
00:32:24.304 --> 00:32:28.512
So your team worked together on different parts of the project.

554
00:32:28.532 --> 00:32:31.954
Yes, teamwork helped us create a better result.

555
00:32:31.974 --> 00:32:33.277
That is a good point.

556
00:32:33.297 --> 00:32:35.394
Collaboration improves quality.

557
00:32:35.413 --> 00:32:38.823
Absolutely — no one can do anything alone in business.

558
00:32:38.843 --> 00:32:40.415
Let's talk about the process.

559
00:32:40.435 --> 00:32:42.266
How did you start the project?

560
00:32:42.286 --> 00:32:44.647
First, we researched the target audience.

561
00:32:44.667 --> 00:32:46.231
What is target audience?

562
00:32:46.251 --> 00:32:50.194
The target audience is the group of people you want to reach.

563
00:32:50.214 --> 00:32:53.923
For example, customers who might use the product exactly as intended.

564
00:32:53.964 --> 00:32:57.571
In our project, the target audience was busy professionals.

565
00:32:57.592 --> 00:32:58.498
Why busy professionals?

566
00:32:58.762 --> 00:33:01.784
The application helps people manage their time.

567
00:33:01.804 --> 00:33:02.548
That makes sense.

568
00:33:02.567 --> 00:33:05.855
Busy professionals often need productivity tools.

569
00:33:05.875 --> 00:33:10.175
So we focused our marketing messages on time management benefits.

570
00:33:10.195 --> 00:33:11.954
What did you do after research?

571
00:33:11.974 --> 00:33:15.007
After research, we planned the marketing strategy.

572
00:33:15.027 --> 00:33:18.826
Strategy means the plan you follow to achieve your goal.

573
00:33:18.846 --> 00:33:22.634
Our strategy was to highlight the product's advantages.

574
00:33:22.653 --> 00:33:24.925
What advantages did you emphasize?

575
00:33:24.945 --> 00:33:28.232
We emphasized ease of use and time-saving features.

576
00:33:28.252 --> 00:33:31.288
That sounds like a strong marketing approach.

577
00:33:31.307 --> 00:33:34.594
Yes, customers want solutions that make life easier.

578
00:33:34.614 --> 00:33:36.369
How long did the project take?

579
00:33:36.390 --> 00:33:37.644
It took about three weeks.

580
00:33:37.663 --> 00:33:41.461
That is a reasonable time for a marketing project.

581
00:33:41.480 --> 00:33:44.765
We needed time to research, plan, and create content.

582
00:33:44.785 --> 00:33:46.800
Did you face any challenges?

583
00:33:46.820 --> 00:33:50.362
If every project has challenges, what challenges did you face?

584
00:33:50.382 --> 00:33:53.923
One challenge was finding the right marketing message.

585
00:33:53.942 --> 00:33:55.237
Why was that difficult?

586
00:33:55.257 --> 00:33:58.923
Because different customers value different benefits.

587
00:33:58.942 --> 00:33:59.713
For example.

588
00:33:59.733 --> 00:34:03.925
Some customers care about price, and others care about features.

589
00:34:03.945 --> 00:34:07.606
We needed a message that appealed to many customers.

590
00:34:07.626 --> 00:34:09.452
How did you solve that problem?

591
00:34:09.472 --> 00:34:13.664
We discussed ideas as a team and tested different messages.

592
00:34:13.684 --> 00:34:16.034
Testing ideas is a good strategy.

593
00:34:16.054 --> 00:34:17.875
Feedback helps improve results.

594
00:34:17.894 --> 00:34:18.931
What is feedback?

595
00:34:18.951 --> 00:34:25.811
Feedback is information about performance or quality, for example, comments from colleagues or customers.

596
00:34:25.831 --> 00:34:26.302
Exactly.

597
00:34:26.322 --> 00:34:28.407
Feedback helps you learn and improve.

598
00:34:28.427 --> 00:34:31.039
Did you receive feedback on your project?

599
00:34:31.059 --> 00:34:33.672
Yes, we received feedback from our manager.

600
00:34:33.693 --> 00:34:35.248
Was the feedback positive?

601
00:34:35.268 --> 00:34:37.880
It was both positive and constructive.

602
00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:40.250
What does constructive feedback mean?

603
00:34:40.269 --> 00:34:44.197
Constructive feedback helps improvement in a helpful way.

604
00:34:44.217 --> 00:34:45.385
That sounds useful.

605
00:34:45.405 --> 00:34:45.782
Yes.

606
00:34:45.802 --> 00:34:49.202
Feedback is important for professional growth.

607
00:34:49.222 --> 00:34:50.519
Let's talk about results.

608
00:34:50.539 --> 00:34:52.885
What was the outcome of the project?

609
00:34:52.905 --> 00:34:53.943
The outcome was positive.

610
00:34:53.963 --> 00:34:57.139
The advertisement campaign attracted attention.

611
00:34:57.159 --> 00:34:58.632
That sounds successful.

612
00:35:00.202 --> 00:35:02.297
Responses from potential customers.

613
00:35:02.317 --> 00:35:03.282
Did sales increase?

614
00:35:03.302 --> 00:35:05.250
It was a marketing project.

615
00:35:05.269 --> 00:35:07.217
So we focused on awareness.

616
00:35:07.237 --> 00:35:10.907
Awareness means customers knowing about the product exactly.

617
00:35:10.927 --> 00:35:14.347
Awareness is the first step in marketing that makes sense.

618
00:35:14.367 --> 00:35:18.028
Customers need to know about a product before buying it.

619
00:35:18.048 --> 00:35:20.246
Yes, marketing helps communicate value.

620
00:35:20.266 --> 00:35:22.949
Let's talk about what you learned from the project.

621
00:35:22.969 --> 00:35:28.362
I learned many things about teamwork and communication that is important in business.

622
00:35:28.382 --> 00:35:31.063
Yes, communication helps teams work effectively.

623
00:35:31.083 --> 00:35:32.291
What else did you learn?

624
00:35:32.311 --> 00:35:34.753
I learned how to analyze customer needs.

625
00:35:34.773 --> 00:35:36.230
That is a valuable skill.

626
00:35:36.250 --> 00:35:40.405
Businesses must understand customer needs to create good solutions.

627
00:35:40.425 --> 00:35:43.114
Do you feel the project improved your skills?

628
00:35:43.134 --> 00:35:47.293
Yes, it helped me improve marketing and problem-solving skills.

629
00:35:47.313 --> 00:35:49.751
That sounds like professional growth.

630
00:35:49.771 --> 00:35:50.244
Exactly.

631
00:35:50.264 --> 00:35:52.702
Every project is an opportunity to learn.

632
00:35:52.722 --> 00:35:56.498
Now let's practice explaining the project in simple English.

633
00:35:56.518 --> 00:35:57.039
Good idea.

634
00:35:57.059 --> 00:35:58.443
I will give a short explanation.

635
00:35:58.860 --> 00:36:02.280
Okay, the project was about marketing a mobile application.

636
00:36:02.300 --> 00:36:02.500
My role was to create ideas for the advertisement.

637
00:36:02.561 --> 00:36:07.530
I worked with a team to research customers and plan marketing messages.

638
00:36:07.550 --> 00:36:11.025
We focused on time-saving benefits and ease of use.

639
00:36:11.045 --> 00:36:14.527
The project took three weeks and received positive feedback.

640
00:36:14.547 --> 00:36:17.492
I learned about teamwork and communication.

641
00:36:17.512 --> 00:36:20.992
That was a great explanation, clear and professional.

642
00:36:21.012 --> 00:36:21.530
Thank you.

643
00:36:21.550 --> 00:36:25.025
Simple language works well in business communication.

644
00:36:25.045 --> 00:36:27.985
Now let's summarize what we discussed today.

645
00:36:28.005 --> 00:36:31.751
We talked about how to explain a project in English.

646
00:36:31.771 --> 00:36:38.487
We discussed project goals, roles, challenges and results, and we used simple business English phrases.

647
00:36:38.507 --> 00:36:42.519
Explaining projects is an important professional skill.

648
00:36:42.539 --> 00:36:46.286
Yes, it helps communication in interviews and meetings.

649
00:36:46.306 --> 00:36:48.177
Now it's your turn to practice.

650
00:36:48.197 --> 00:36:50.061
Here is a question for you.

651
00:36:50.081 --> 00:36:51.978
Can you explain a project you worked on?

652
00:36:51.998 --> 00:36:55.056
Try to describe the project in simple English.

653
00:36:56.467 --> 00:36:59.275
You can mention the goal, your role and the results.

654
00:36:59.295 --> 00:37:01.105
Any practice is good practice.

655
00:37:01.125 --> 00:37:02.958
Thank you for joining us today.

656
00:37:05.188 --> 00:37:13.199
Hope you'll have a great day, and we'll see you next time.

657
00:37:13.219 --> 00:37:14.344
Goodbye, everyone.

658
00:37:14.364 --> 00:37:14.940
Bye.
